From the engine-half critic (2026-07-18) — CLIENT-HALF obligations the endpoint cannot enforce:
(a) flush ONLY records whose Rung.verify() already passed (server trusts caller; the runner's guard is the gate);
(b) flush from holder.unflushed(), never a full re-send of completions();
(c) retire ladder.db/CompletionStore AND lower tests/test_no_new_sqlite.py census in the SAME commit (D6 ratchet);
(d) HttpLadderStore defines DEFAULT_TENANT='default' matching TenantConstants (the 5x-repeated http_*_store convention);
(e) VERIFIED_AT SEMANTICS (pin in HttpLadderStore's module docstring so nobody later 'fixes' it): the flush re-records via the engine upsert which SERVER-stamps verified_at=now() at FLUSH time — the holder's buffered verify-moment timestamp is DISCARDED by design (RF-186-2 observability-only/lossy). Also: client isoformat(+00:00, seconds) vs Java OffsetDateTime.toString(Z, microseconds) — both parse via datetime.fromisoformat, nothing compares them; known, accepted.
Also noted: no delete/reset surface is CORRECT minimalism (CompletionLedger has no delete; nothing ever deletes a completion); Gap-4 clean on the Java side but only docstring-enforced there (the AST pin scans Python only — cross-language enforcement out of .12 scope, noted for the epic).

description:
Field evidence (Steve, 2026-07-11, second user to hit this class): nx migrate-to-service pre-gates on 5 pre-RDR-108 legacy-chunk-ID collections, and the remediation today is a MANUAL multi-step runbook: back up the Chroma dir, salvage knowledge__* text via a direct chromadb.PersistentClient snippet (nx CLI verbs route to the service in service mode, not Chroma), delete the blocked collections from Chroma, re-run migrate, then re-index source repos + nx taxonomy discover + re-store salvaged notes. Every step is a place an unsupervised Claude can go wrong (GH #1390: one dropped the chash constraints to force upserts through).

Proposal: a single command (nx migration remediate, name TBD) that, for each pre-gate-blocked collection:
1. classifies it: derived (taxonomy__centroids -> regenerate later), repo-derived (rdr__/code__/docs__ with a resolvable source repo -> re-index later), or note-shaped (knowledge__* -> salvage text now);
2. salvages note-shaped content to a local JSON artifact (documents + metadatas) before any delete;
3. archives-and-removes the blocked collections from the Chroma source (copy-not-move: an archive dir or the existing backup convention);
4. emits a post-migration rebuild plan (the exact nx index repo / nx taxonomy discover / nx store put commands), and optionally executes it after the ETL completes;
5. keeps the GH #1390 rule intact: never touches the pgvector chash constraints.

The pre-gate already has all classification inputs (detection.py CollectionClassification carries legacy_ids, model, counts). The runbook lives in the recovery gist (08f0a615e3d73e47d8062bce4829b611 Phase 4b) and should become the command's spec. Blog post 00 (Installing Nexus) now sets the honest expectation and can link the command once it exists.

Incident 2026-07-04: T2 nexus/deployed-engine-version sat at v0.1.17 while 0.1.18/0.1.19/0.1.22 deployed unrecorded; plan-audit for nexus-duoak consumed the stale value and baked a wrong fact into the duoak.1 bead (caught only because the main session challenged it against live /version). Class: dead feedback loop / system-of-record rot (same family as the 2026-06→07 service-target postmortem and RDR-179's drift-audit findings).

DEEPER FIX (this bead): make the tracker update MECHANICAL, part of the deploy pipeline, not a memory-discipline hope. Candidate shapes (pick during RDR-179 remediation):
(a) engine-release skill / deploy-relay checklist gains a hard final step: curl /version, assert == just-deployed tag, memory_put the tracker — relay is NOT complete until written;
(b) better: eliminate the cache for decisions — a nx helper (e.g. nx engine version --live) that always hits /version, and grep-audit consumers of the T2 entry to route through it;
(c) cloud-gate script (STEP-6) writes the tracker itself as a side effect of passing, so the record updates exactly when the deploy is validated.

Interim mitigation already in place: verification rule written into the T2 entry itself (tracker = cache; curl before any version-dependent decision). Attach to RDR-179 remediation arc when it opens (docs/rdr/rdr-179; gate not yet run).

RDR-187 §Approach STEP 4 (conexus lockstep — THEIRS, all mechanical). NO code in this repo. Action = surface an explicit "relay" to Hal of the T2 conexus [21000] inventory, then AWAIT conexus confirmation the edits are filed/landed in lockstep with our view-changelog / DROP.

BLOCKS the DROP (B5): the deployed diag conformance view (nexus.diag_chash_conformance, conexus-3ilh — their DDL) and their rekey driver's inline conformance SQL are a generator-pinned COPY of the same object; if the table drops before their leg drops, their verify tooling errors on a missing relation.

Their five direct nexus.chash_index readers ([21000], all conexus-owned):
1) deploy/verify/restore_rowcount.sql:44 rowcount leg;
2) deploy/migrate/rekey_chash_window.py CHECK_TABLES/GRANDFATHERED_CHECK_TABLES + conformance SQL + read_counts (the 292,230 grandfathered debt ceiling evaporates with the table);
3) deploy/migrate/provision_diag_path.py view_ddl chash_index leg;
4) deploy/migrate/bootstrap-engine-db.sh:681,688 conditional view creation (8-table gate + DDL leg);
5) deploy/gate/rdr164_cascade_explain_probe.py:38 retarget — mirrors CatalogRepository.deleteCollection cascade; changes in LOCKSTEP with our cascade-leg drop in B5.
Plus two backend-switch doc copy-edits (ROLLBACK.md, SERVICE_COVERAGE.md). cutover_smoke.py goes via /v1/chash/ and survives by design.

